<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-30.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="024144"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#024144" label="[02:41:44]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: Yeah, this is one reason (of many) I wrote Onramp. I'm not sure there's much value in going from C to Scheme back to C; the mescc detour is really complicated and I couldn't get anything working when I tried it (granted this was a couple years ago so I'm sure it has improved a lot.) Onramp's bootstrap is overall a lot simpler</span></div><div class="line" id="024237"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#024237" label="[02:42:37]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I've heard suggestions that bootstrapping from C to Scheme to C improves security but I think this is a misunderstanding of diverse double compilation</span></div><div class="line" id="025113"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025113" label="[02:51:13]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Although if by &quot;quicker&quot; you mean just in the time it takes, it's almost certainly not, because Onramp is really slow. But it's simpler, more portable and less code</span></div><div class="line" id="044248"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#044248" label="[04:42:48]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Took about 15 minutes on my ryzen 3700X desktop, seems quite faster than what I remember of live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="050643"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#050643" label="[05:06:43]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Also wrote a C implementation of the shell <a rel="nofollow" href="https://hacktivis.me/tmp/onramp_sh-oe.c">https://hacktivis.me/tmp/onramp_sh-oe.c</a>  compiles to 27K with `x86_64-pc-linux-musl-clang -std=c99 -Os -s -static`</span></div><div class="line" id="050716"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#050716" label="[05:07:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm heading towards pentium-m single core clocked down to 600Mhz</span></div><div class="line" id="051054"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#051054" label="[05:10:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although i'm happy smp seems stable now, md-raid doesn't panic anymore either... needs alot of runtime testing still</span></div><div class="line" id="051158"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#051158" label="[05:11:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if anytime all the fun moved to some FPGA, a single-core 600Mhz is rather luxurious (FPGA is expensive), i rather ensure bootstrapping/compilation are tailored to this</span></div><div class="line" id="051613"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#051613" label="[05:16:13]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think I'd more see ~600MHz x86_32 as interesting for full non-hardware-accelerated emulated virtual machines like 86Box.</span></div><div class="line" id="052503"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#052503" label="[05:25:03]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">several years later: bootstrap computer from stone tools :)</span></div><div class="line" id="052531"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#052531" label="[05:25:31]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: Wow that's awesome! Thanks for implementing that! Does it work with the Onramp build scripts? This is going to be really helpful for debugging them. I'm going to try it now</span></div><div class="line" id="053017"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#053017" label="[05:30:17]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Wow it works. Well done. I'm surprised there aren't more bugs in my build scripts, I didn't actually have an implementation of the Onramp shell to test them until now</span></div><div class="line" id="053049"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#053049" label="[05:30:49]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I did get this error when it got to the libc/common build script: `mkdir: error: Failed creating directory: build/output/include/__onramp`</span></div><div class="line" id="053144"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#053144" label="[05:31:44]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah yeah I have some mkdir calls prior in the init.c, the cmd_mkdir() implementation is incomplete</span></div><div class="line" id="053245"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#053245" label="[05:32:45]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">namely, build build/intermediate build/intermediate/hex-0-onramp build/intermediate/sh build/output/ build/output/include</span></div><div class="line" id="053351"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#053351" label="[05:33:51]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It's funny, in an older version of the shell spec, I had `mkdir -p` not be recursive, the -p was just to prevent errors on existing directories when running with a POSIX shell</span></div><div class="line" id="053408"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#053408" label="[05:34:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: an FPGA with 600MHz and sufficient amount logic-cells for x86_32 is expensive</span></div><div class="line" id="053437"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#053437" label="[05:34:37]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I've read that</span></div><div class="line" id="053439"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#053439" label="[05:34:39]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah yeah that could probably help on keeping a small implementation, recursive mkdir being a bit big</span></div><div class="line" id="053441"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#053441" label="[05:34:41]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And all the build scripts manually created all the intermediate directories, but with -p, which just looked too wrong. So I decided to change it, figured I'd have to bite the bullet and make a recursive mkdir in the shell</span></div><div class="line" id="053455"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#053455" label="[05:34:55]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Could always change it back...</span></div><div class="line" id="053511"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#053511" label="[05:35:11]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Maybe I'll see how hard it is to implement in bytecode</span></div><div class="line" id="053540"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#053540" label="[05:35:40]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Found it, here's the commit: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ludocode/onramp/commit/aa942bbacd3c3024d60e38206281a766dc3e3c39">https://github.com/ludocode/onramp/commit/aa942bbacd3c3024d60e38206281a766dc3e3c39</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="053906"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#053906" label="[05:39:06]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah yeah with each build.sh having ~2 more mkdir calls it does makes things bigger</span></div><div class="line" id="054221"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#054221" label="[05:42:21]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah. I was mostly concerned with getting bug reports about the scripts being obviously wrong since -p is supposed to be recursive :)</span></div><div class="line" id="060504"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#060504" label="[06:05:04]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">grabbed recursive mkdir from my utils, doesn't seems too bad: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://hacktivis.me/tmp/onramp_sh-oe_1.c">https://hacktivis.me/tmp/onramp_sh-oe_1.c</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="061444"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#061444" label="[06:14:44]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Also relicensed it to MIT, like onramp is</span></div><div class="line" id="065806"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#065806" label="[06:58:06]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Nice, the bootstrap works flawlessly with your shell</span></div><div class="line" id="065911"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#065911" label="[06:59:11]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">FYI you can setup just the VM and other platform-specific stuff with `scripts/posix/build.sh --setup`. Then running your shell on `core/build.sh` completes the bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="071556"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#071556" label="[07:15:56]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well right now I build hex in make-root.sh and init.c then builds /bin/onrampvm and build/intermediate/hex-0-onramp/hex.oe</span></div><div class="line" id="071644"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#071644" label="[07:16:44]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And yeah after that it launches /bin/sh-oe core/build.sh</span></div><div class="line" id="073640"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#073640" label="[07:36:40]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Nice. Glad to hear it works</span></div><div class="line" id="073704"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#073704" label="[07:37:04]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm still some number of months away from getting it building TinyCC but I'm hoping to get there ASAP.</span></div><div class="line" id="113315"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#113315" label="[11:33:15]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well the expensive part is the 600Mhz not the x86_32 core.</span></div><div class="line" id="145157"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145157" label="[14:51:57]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hello! I heard rumors that having a simple-to-build .xz file decompressor might be useful here.</span></div><div class="line" id="145313"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145313" label="[14:53:13]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I made one from XZ Embedded. (A variant of that is in Linux kernel too.)</span></div><div class="line" id="145319"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145319" label="[14:53:19]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://tukaani.org/xz/xzminidec_standalone_2024-12-30.c">https://tukaani.org/xz/xzminidec_standalone_2024-12-30.c</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="145410"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145410" label="[14:54:10]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It builds even with -std=gnu89 so I suppose there's a chance to make it build with limited compilers.</span></div><div class="line" id="145450"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145450" label="[14:54:50]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">If it needs tweaking, please don't edit that file. Use <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/tukaani-project/xz-embedded">https://github.com/tukaani-project/xz-embedded</a>  instead, there's a script to create the above .c file.</span></div><div class="line" id="145511"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#145511" label="[14:55:11]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Sorry for the noise if I understood the rumors wrong. :)</span></div><div class="line" id="152542"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#152542" label="[15:25:42]">&lt;efraim&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm going to try pulling that in for guix's bootstrap, waiting for the long decompression on some tarballs is enough to try it out</span></div><div class="line" id="152706"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#152706" label="[15:27:06]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">xzminidec is slower than xz, especially on x86-64 now that there is assembly code. But the C code in xzminidec was slower too (but more readable).</span></div><div class="line" id="152827"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#152827" label="[15:28:27]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">For bootstrapping I suppose the difference isn't relevant.</span></div><div class="line" id="153055"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153055" label="[15:30:55]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Anyway, thanks for testing. :) I'll step away for an hour or three now.</span></div><div class="line" id="153056"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#153056" label="[15:30:56]">&lt;efraim&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think we use bootar for our initial tar/bzip2/gzip/xz <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.ngyro.com/bootar">https://git.ngyro.com/bootar</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="153106"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153106" label="[15:31:06]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Oh, I'll check now</span></div><div class="line" id="153459"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153459" label="[15:34:59]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah, there is the whole decompressor written in Scheme.</span></div><div class="line" id="153857"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153857" label="[15:38:57]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">efraim: Did I understand correctly that you hope xzminidec (C code) to be faster than the Scheme implementation? At that point of bootstrap you have a C compiler? Sorry for silly questions.</span></div><div class="line" id="153951"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#153951" label="[15:39:51]">&lt;efraim&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we get a C compiler a few steps later, at which point we could use a faster xz decompressor</span></div><div class="line" id="154054"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#154054" label="[15:40:54]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">OK :)</span></div><div class="line" id="173549"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#173549" label="[17:35:49]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: the reason mescc is written in scheme is mostly because writing a scheme interpreter is simpler</span></div><div class="line" id="173616"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#173616" label="[17:36:16]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Or, I shouldn't say &quot;the reason&quot;, I'm not in the dev's brain</span></div><div class="line" id="173636"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#173636" label="[17:36:36]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But that's the main benefit afaik</span></div><div class="line" id="174354"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174354" label="[17:43:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: we have xz decompressor in mescc-tools-extra</span></div><div class="line" id="174442"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174442" label="[17:44:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/mescc-tools-extra/blob/master/unxz.c">https://github.com/oriansj/mescc-tools-extra/blob/master/unxz.c</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="174518"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#174518" label="[17:45:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is there any difference between unlzma and unxz?</span></div><div class="line" id="174554"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#174554" label="[17:45:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">iv'e just stumbled upon a collision between what busybox-1.2.2.1 got and the additional userspace such as xz-utils merged</span></div><div class="line" id="174628"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#174628" label="[17:46:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">busybox seems a decent place to bundle (most) archival utilities into</span></div><div class="line" id="174634"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174634" label="[17:46:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's probably a symlink</span></div><div class="line" id="174647"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174647" label="[17:46:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though lzma and xz  are sligthtly different containers</span></div><div class="line" id="174701"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#174701" label="[17:47:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah, ok.</span></div><div class="line" id="174800"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174800" label="[17:48:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: that unxz is based on some other xz implementation, possibly libarchive, but adjusted to be buildable with very simplistic M2-Planet compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="174834"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174834" label="[17:48:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so that we get xz support really early in the bootstrap chain</span></div><div class="line" id="174847"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174847" label="[17:48:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(way before we have C99 compiler such as tcc)</span></div><div class="line" id="174920"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#174920" label="[17:49:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">any chance busybox source could be digested by mesc?</span></div><div class="line" id="174921"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174921" label="[17:49:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: I think you added that unxz?</span></div><div class="line" id="174933"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#174933" label="[17:49:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unlikely</span></div><div class="line" id="175052"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#175052" label="[17:50:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no problem</span></div><div class="line" id="175130"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#175130" label="[17:51:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it would also take ages to build</span></div><div class="line" id="175144"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#175144" label="[17:51:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mescc takes maybe 10 minutes on fast x86_64 machine to build tcc</span></div><div class="line" id="175750"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#175750" label="[17:57:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in this case it's not a task done each day, bootstrapping a C-compiler with some scheme interpreter</span></div><div class="line" id="180222"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#180222" label="[18:02:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">meanwhile i could isolate anothre root cause for various linux24 kernel panics during boot with SMP, that is some of those were/are timer-subsystem related</span></div><div class="line" id="180435"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#180435" label="[18:04:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although my system-profile with tinycc is sufficiently sanitized to fit into a nosmp performance envelope, it's a nice smp is available again since last night</span></div><div class="line" id="180558"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#180558" label="[18:05:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently i consider backporting various things from linux2.6/3.x back onto linux2.4 a feasible approach</span></div><div class="line" id="180756"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#180756" label="[18:07:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">situation with kernel now is, it isn't trapped into GNU-toolchain and does permit an alternative system-integration for a fully functional development host, optional</span></div><div class="line" id="181300"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#181300" label="[18:13:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">thing is, for example, for an ao486 fpga deployment tinycore linux exists, but this system wouldn't be self-hosting on a resource-constrained free-hardware FPGA system</span></div><div class="line" id="181358"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#181358" label="[18:13:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you could run such software on the type of free-hardware, but i couldn't imagine to cope with gcc/g++ on a single-core/nosmp system to maintain a complete distribution including bootstrapping from source</span></div><div class="line" id="181400"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#181400" label="[18:14:00]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: That unxz.c is obviously based on LZMA SDK. The SDK is the upstream of LZMA code. :)</span></div><div class="line" id="181551"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#181551" label="[18:15:51]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: I didn't test but I would guess that unxz.c to be a little faster than xzminidec.</span></div><div class="line" id="181635"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#181635" label="[18:16:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: hmm, haven't tested though it is definitely far slower than proper xz</span></div><div class="line" id="181645"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#181645" label="[18:16:45]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I haven't looked at BusyBox source in many years. I helped a bit with adding XZ Embedded and thus unxz tool into BusyBox well over ten years ago.</span></div><div class="line" id="181656"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#181656" label="[18:16:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also if you build with M2-Planet, it will be even slower....</span></div><div class="line" id="181715"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#181715" label="[18:17:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hasn't xz been designed for multi-core parallelism?</span></div><div class="line" id="181815"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#181815" label="[18:18:15]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: is that available outside of github?</span></div><div class="line" id="181854"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#181854" label="[18:18:54]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: With big files, yes. It just splits the file in pieces and processes them in parallel.</span></div><div class="line" id="181901"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#181901" label="[18:19:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">interesting aspect with de/compression, most algorithms miss the sweet-spot between compression-ratio and runtime-performance anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="181917"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#181917" label="[18:19:17]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: Which makes compression worse. Usually it's not significant but one can have cases where it's significant.</span></div><div class="line" id="181954"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#181954" label="[18:19:54]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: git clone <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.tukaani.org/xz-embedded.git">https://git.tukaani.org/xz-embedded.git</a>   or browse at <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.tukaani.org/?p=xz-embedded.git">https://git.tukaani.org/?p=xz-embedded.git</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="182004"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#182004" label="[18:20:04]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: thanks</span></div><div class="line" id="182015"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182015" label="[18:20:15]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: Short home page: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://tukaani.org/xz/embedded.html">https://tukaani.org/xz/embedded.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="182047"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#182047" label="[18:20:47]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with regards to runtime performance: gzip, xz and various other aren't the best choice anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="182131"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182131" label="[18:21:31]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: In 2005, I found LZMA hitting that sweetspot really well. It was much faster to decompress than bzip2. But nowadays with faster connections, zstd hits the spot better.</span></div><div class="line" id="182230"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#182230" label="[18:22:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lz4 seemed closest to the sweet spot with the compression-ratio and runtime-performance iirc, maybe lz77 too but i've not tested this one</span></div><div class="line" id="182230"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182230" label="[18:22:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, zstd wasn't available back then</span></div><div class="line" id="182246"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182246" label="[18:22:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and also a lot of software on the bootstrap path to GCC are from early 2000</span></div><div class="line" id="182305"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#182305" label="[18:23:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i've blacklisted zstd on gentoo due to system-integration issues and it's dependency graph</span></div><div class="line" id="182306"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#182306" label="[18:23:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so they mostly provite tar.gz and tar.bz2 with just occasional tar.xz once we get to newer software</span></div><div class="line" id="182446"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182446" label="[18:24:46]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Is lz77 a tool? I know LZ77 is principle behind a lot of compressors (not in bzip2 though).</span></div><div class="line" id="182518"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#182518" label="[18:25:18]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: stage0-posix does now include an XZ decompressor, derived from muxzcat, but a more &quot;official&quot; one is still welcome</span></div><div class="line" id="182542"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182542" label="[18:25:42]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: lz4 is awesome for some tasks but for distributing packages it's not.</span></div><div class="line" id="182617"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182617" label="[18:26:17]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: LZMA SDK is official too. muxzcat I don't know, there's a lot I haven't heard about.</span></div><div class="line" id="182628"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#182628" label="[18:26:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: lz77 was the earliest compressors available, decades ago, but wasn't widely adopted as a &quot;standard&quot;, and who knows, patent issues, don't recall the details</span></div><div class="line" id="182631"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182631" label="[18:26:31]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: Whatever works well is good. :)</span></div><div class="line" id="182648"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182648" label="[18:26:48]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: gzip, LZMA, lz4, ... are all LZ77.</span></div><div class="line" id="182735"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#182735" label="[18:27:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: that's not how i looked at it, i've merely evaluated the runtime-performance/compression-ratio balance, and if a stable imlementation exists for any</span></div><div class="line" id="182740"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#182740" label="[18:27:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">imo, lz4 was the best</span></div><div class="line" id="182807"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182807" label="[18:28:07]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: Back in 2005, even if zstd had been available, LZMA could have been better. If you had 256 kbit/s download speed, smaller file saved more time than slower decompression took.</span></div><div class="line" id="182914"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182914" label="[18:29:14]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: Different use cases have different sweetspots. For example, I think of &quot;compress once, decompress many times&quot; case when distributing software packages.</span></div><div class="line" id="183010"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#183010" label="[18:30:10]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: which introduces another tradeoff, in practice, ideally, sticking to one and only one algorithm/implementation available on every platform, from tiniest embedded up to hpc workstations</span></div><div class="line" id="183025"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#183025" label="[18:30:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gzip, xz and various other are far distant to any ideal</span></div><div class="line" id="183123"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#183123" label="[18:31:23]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gzip wasn't too bad, if _all_ distfiles were distributed with this container format, but they aren't</span></div><div class="line" id="183400"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#183400" label="[18:34:00]">&lt;nimaje&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I would understand lz77 as reference to the paper &quot;A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression&quot; (Lempel Ziv 1977), which seems to only define the base compression algorithm</span></div><div class="line" id="183600"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#183600" label="[18:36:00]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: gzip uses Deflate which uses 32 KiB LZ77 dictionary followed by Huffman coding. If I had to guess, the 32 KiB limit might have something to do with 16-bit CPUs making it slower to handle buffers bigger than 64 KiB (the encoder data structures are bigger than just the dictionary).</span></div><div class="line" id="183650"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#183650" label="[18:36:50]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: zstd and LZMA allow far bigger dictionaries = history buffers, so they can find more repeated sequences.</span></div><div class="line" id="183743"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#183743" label="[18:37:43]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nimaje: Yes</span></div><div class="line" id="183910"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#183910" label="[18:39:10]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">LZ4 can use up to 64 KiB dictionary. One could extend the format to support more, of course. So it would be a new format again. ;)</span></div><div class="line" id="184203"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184203" label="[18:42:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">5 years ago i was searching for any compressor implementation to apply some LFSR scrambling with it, to remain fast and efficient</span></div><div class="line" id="184239"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184239" label="[18:42:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i picked LZ4 because quality implementation existed, in linux kernel and userspace</span></div><div class="line" id="184249"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#184249" label="[18:42:49]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I downloaded unxz.c and also M2libc/bootstrappable.[hc] but it didn't compile due to uint32_t* being assigned to uint8_t*. Changing that made it build but it doesn't work.</span></div><div class="line" id="184356"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#184356" label="[18:43:56]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: As I said, LZ4 is awesome for many things. I just think it's not for package distribution. gzip makes smaller files, yes, slower, but one needs really fast download speed to compensate for bigger file size at that point.</span></div><div class="line" id="184424"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#184424" label="[18:44:24]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: So you chose well, there just are many use cases and thus many compressors exist. :)</span></div><div class="line" id="184532"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#184532" label="[18:45:32]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: btw while I think about it, might make sense to use a non-printable character (like how ELF files use DEL) for onramp bytecode format, specially as with the wrap-header feature means it's not always at start of the file.</span></div><div class="line" id="185023"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#185023" label="[18:50:23]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: Re: &quot;so they mostly provite tar.gz and tar.bz2&quot; -- From trust point of view, I don't understand why one couldn't decompress before starting the bootstrap. I know it's about trusting only the original files but... somehow you have to get the original files.</span></div><div class="line" id="190524"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#190524" label="[19:05:24]">&lt;Larhzu&gt;</a></span><span class="message">With the pointer type fix, unxz.c works for tiny files.</span></div><div class="line" id="190608"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#190608" label="[19:06:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: yeah, in principle from the trust point of view decompressed files are fine</span></div><div class="line" id="190619"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#190619" label="[19:06:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just inconvenient to distribute</span></div><div class="line" id="190655"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#190655" label="[19:06:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Larhzu: alternatively, you could (recursively clone stage0-posix) and run `make test-amd64`</span></div><br /></div></body></html>